**Kali Linux Virtual Machine**
•	Created a Kali Linux VM in VMware Workstation using an ISO installer from kali.org
•	Configured the VM for approximately 4GB RAM, 2 vCPU cores, NAT networking, and a growable virtual disk (60GB recommended during setup; final disk capacity not independently verified). This is going to depend on how much capacity your Workstation has.
•	Installed Kali using hostname kali-attacker; left DNS domain field blank; created a local user account.
•	Used guided partitioning on the VMware virtual disk; approved writing partition changes and installed GRUB to the virtual boot disk.
•	Selected the Xfce desktop and Kali tool collection during installation.
•	Updated package metadata and installed upgrades using apt; accepted automatic service restarts during package upgrades.
•	Installed/open-vm-tools-desktop was recommended and a reboot followed; exact command completion not independently confirmed.
•	A clean VMware snapshot was recommended; creation was not confirmed.
Kali commands used 
•	sudo apt update – update you Kali Machine after downloading.
•	sudo apt full-upgrade -y – Upgrade all the upgradable packages.
•	sudo reboot -  restart the system to apply updtaes and changes.
•	ip -br a – check your connections
•	ping -c 4 kali.org
